Correction: the page transcription above is incomplete. Here is the full page:

<page type="text">
  <layout>One page of text: a centred verse block at the top; below it a closing dedication with the signature "J. Iacomotus B."; then a printed woodcut ornament; catchword "AD" at the bottom right. No running head, no page number.</layout>
  <main_text>
    <p><i>Marte</i> <i>feros meditetur hostes</i>
<i>Pulsare iusto: seu</i> PATRIÆ <i>bonas</i>
<i>Seruare paces cogitet, & suos</i>
<i>Ditare subiectos verendus</i>
<i>Iustitia moderante</i> PRINCEPS.
<i>Cui sacra dono,</i> DUX BONE, <i>si faues</i>
<i>Lamenta</i> HEBRÆO <i>carmine quæ prius</i>
MYSTES <i>Jonauit. Sacra non sunt</i>
<i>Consiliis nocitura rectis.</i></p>
    <p>Vestræ Celsitudini deditissimus</p>
    <p><i>J. Iacomotus B.</i></p>
  </main_text>
  <bottom_margin>
    <catchword>AD</catchword>
  </bottom_margin>
  <notes>The verse block is set in italic, with names and some words in roman capitals (MARTE, PATRIÆ, PRINCEPS, DUX BONE, HEBRÆO, MYSTES); "Marte" is in small capitals in the scan. The woodcut ornament below the signature (a cherub-like head between foliage and scrolls with pendant fringes) is not text and is not transcribed. "AD" at the bottom right is the catchword. Some italic line-breaks within the verse may not match the exact printed positions; the wording itself is as printed.</notes>
</page>